/-!
# The lifted presentation algebra of a graded deformation

Let `P` be a graded `k`-algebra with a weighted-homogeneous presentation

`k[X_i] ⟶ P`,  `X_i ↦ x_i ∈ 𝒜 (d i)`,  relations `F_j` of degree `e j`,

let `A` be a square-zero thickening of `k` and let `Q` be a deformation of `P` over `A` with
reduction `red : Q → P`. Choose homogeneous lifts `y_i ∈ 𝒬 (d i)` of the generators. This file
builds the algebra which presents `Q` itself:

`S = A[X_i] / (R_j)`,

where each `R_j` is a weighted-homogeneous polynomial over `A` of degree `e j` which reduces to
`F_j` and which *vanishes at the chosen lifts*, `R_j(y) = 0`. Such a lift of the relations
exists: `F_j(y)` is killed by the reduction, so it is a multiple of the square-zero ideal, and
every such multiple is already the value at `y` of a polynomial whose coefficients lie in that
ideal. Subtracting that polynomial from an arbitrary homogeneous lift of `F_j` gives `R_j`.

The algebra `S` carries the grading by the images of the weighted-homogeneous submodules, it maps
to `Q` by evaluation at the lifts, and that map satisfies the two conditions of
`Deformation.Examples.ProjectiveGradedAlgebra.bijective_of_gradedPresentation`: it is surjective
onto each graded piece of `P` after reduction, and an element of `S_d` reduced to zero already
lies in `I · S_d`. The second is the substantive one. An element of `S_d` killed by the reduction
is represented by a weighted-homogeneous `u` over `A` of degree `d` whose reduction lies in the
relation ideal of `P`; writing that reduction as a combination of the `F_j` and lifting the
coefficients arbitrarily, `u` differs from an element of the relation ideal of `S` by a
polynomial all of whose coefficients lie in the square-zero ideal. Taking degree-`d` weighted
homogeneous components of that identity keeps the first summand in the relation ideal — it is
homogeneous — and keeps the second a combination of the square-zero ideal with homogeneous
polynomials of degree `d`.

## Main definitions

* `GradedDeformation.liftedAlgebra`: the algebra `A[X_i]/(R_j)`.
* `GradedDeformation.liftedGrading`: its grading by the images of the weighted-homogeneous
  submodules.
* `GradedDeformation.liftedDecomposition`: that grading is a `DirectSum.Decomposition`.
* `GradedDeformation.liftedMap`: the evaluation map `A[X_i]/(R_j) ⟶ Q` at the chosen lifts.
* `DirectSum.decompositionOfCoeEq`: a family of substructures with the same carriers as a
  decomposing family is itself a decomposition.

## Main results

* `MvPolynomial.exists_isWeightedHomogeneous_map_eq`: a weighted-homogeneous polynomial lifts,
  along a surjection of base rings, to a weighted-homogeneous polynomial of the same degree.
* `MvPolynomial.exists_isWeightedHomogeneous_aeval_eq`: a homogeneous element of a graded algebra
  generated by homogeneous elements is the value of a weighted-homogeneous polynomial of the same
  degree.
* `GradedDeformation.reduction_aeval`: the reduction of the value of a polynomial at lifts is the
  value of its reduction at the reductions of the lifts.
* `GradedDeformation.exists_mem_smul_aeval_eq`: a homogeneous element of the deformation killed by
  the reduction is the value at the lifts of a homogeneous polynomial whose coefficients lie in
  the square-zero ideal.
* `GradedDeformation.exists_relationLift`: **the lifted relations exist.**
* `GradedDeformation.liftedMap_mem_of_mem_liftedGrading`,
  `GradedDeformation.exists_mem_liftedGrading_reduction_eq` and
  `GradedDeformation.mem_smul_liftedGrading_of_reduction_eq_zero`: the three conditions which
  `Deformation.Examples.ProjectiveGradedAlgebra.bijective_of_gradedPresentation` requires of a
  lifted presentation.
* `GradedDeformation.exists_lifts_sub_kerElement` and
  `GradedDeformation.relationFirstOrderPart_eq_sub_sum`: perturbing the chosen lifts by a
  prescribed element of `I ⊗[k] 𝒜 (d i)` changes the first-order parts of the relations by the
  graded Jacobian of that element.
* `GradedDeformation.reduction_liftedMap_eq`: two structural maps out of one lifted presentation
  algebra agree after reduction to the special fibre.
* `GradedDeformation.exists_relationLift_common`: two deformations whose relations have the same
  first-order parts at chosen lifts have a *common* family of lifted relations, hence a common
  lifted presentation algebra. This is what
  `Deformation.Examples.ProjectiveGradedAlgebra.nonempty_fiber_iso_of_common_gradedPresentation`
  consumes.

## Implementation notes

The relations of the lifted presentation are carried as an arbitrary family `R : σ → A[X_i]`
together with the three properties it needs, rather than produced by a choice inside the
definition of the algebra. The algebra, its grading and its structural map are then definitions
with computable content and their lemmas are provable; the choice is isolated in the single
existence statement `GradedDeformation.exists_relationLift`.

`DirectSum.decompositionOfCoeEq` exists because the grading of the quotient is naturally a family
of `Submodule`s — that is the shape the presentation criterion consumes — while
`Stacks.GradedQuotient.decomposition` produces one of `AddSubgroup`s. The two families have the
same carriers but live in different `SetLike` types, so no substitution relates them and the
decomposition has to be transported along the additive isomorphisms of the pieces.

The canonical lift of a relation `F_j` is `F_j` itself with coefficients pushed along `k → A`,
rather than an arbitrary homogeneous lift. That choice is what makes the value of the lift at the
chosen lifts of the generators equal to the value of `F_j` read through the `k`-algebra structure
of the deformation, which is the quantity whose first-order part
`GradedDeformation.relationFirstOrderPart` measures; with an arbitrary lift the two values differ
and the first-order parts could not be used.

`GradedDeformation.exists_relationLift_common` asks for equality of the first-order *parts* of the
relations, in `I ⊗[k] 𝒜 (e j)`, not of the first-order *classes* in `W ⊗[k] V`. Equal classes give
equal parts only after perturbing one family of lifts, since the two differ by an element of the
image of the graded Jacobian; `GradedDeformation.relationFirstOrderPart_eq_sub_sum` is the
statement which performs such a perturbation.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0DVZ](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0DVZ)
-/

